# load-bearing.declarations — call sites whose deletion nothing else would notice.
#
# Checked by `scripts/check-load-bearing.sh` (CI job `load-bearing`, and
# `make pre-push-checks`). If a declared call site disappears, the gate fails.
#
# ── When to declare a call site ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Declare a line when ALL THREE are true:
#
#   1. It wires in a guard, filter, hook, or validator — the line does not do
#      the work itself, it causes the work to happen.
#   2. There is exactly one of it. If the guard is installed in ten places,
#      losing one is visible; losing the only one is not.
#   3. Deleting it leaves the suite GREEN. This is the test. If a test already
#      fails when the line is removed, that test is the gate and a declaration
#      here adds only ceremony. Check by actually deleting the line and running
#      the suite — do not reason about it.
#
# Do NOT declare ordinary code. A declaration is a claim that removal is
# invisible, and that claim is what earns the maintenance cost of the entry.
#
# ── Format ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   path: <repo-relative file>
#   call: <literal snippet that must still appear in that file>
#   why:  <what silently stops happening when it is deleted>
#
# One blank line between stanzas. `call:` is matched as a FIXED STRING anywhere
# on a line, so leading indentation is not part of it and regex metacharacters
# are literal. `why:` is required — a declaration with no stated reason fails
# the gate rather than standing as a silent switch. `#` starts a comment.
#
# Keep `call:` long enough to be unique in its file and short enough to survive
# a reformat: the smallest fragment that is unmistakably this call site.
#
# ── When the gate fires ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Restore the call, or move the declaration to the guard's new home, or delete
# the stanza and say in the PR what covers this now. All three are reviewable
# one-line diffs. That is the whole mechanism: it cannot stop the removal, it
# makes the removal visible.
#
# `bash scripts/check-load-bearing.sh --list` resolves every declaration below
# to a file and line number without judging anything.


path: hooks/pre-push
call: if ! make pre-push-checks 2>&1; then
why:  The only local invocation of every pre-push gate. Delete it and the hook still installs, still runs, and checks nothing.

path: hooks/pre-commit
call: if ! make gitleaks-check 2>&1; then
why:  The only pre-commit secret scan. Delete it and a staged credential reaches the remote before CI's gitleaks-scan sees it.

path: .claude/settings.json
call: .claude/hooks/pre-mr-security-gate.sh
why:  The only wiring of the pre-MR security hook. scripts/tests/pre-mr-security-gate.test.sh tests the script, not that anything calls it.

path: .claude/settings.json
call: .claude/hooks/pre-tool-safety.sh
why:  The only wiring of the Edit/Write safety hook. Removing this entry disarms it while the script itself stays present and passing.

path: .claude/settings.json
call: "Bash(gh pr merge:*)"
why:  The only rule stopping an agent from merging a PR, `--auto` included. Delete it and nothing else in the suite fails: the hooks and skills only tell agents not to merge.

path: scripts/check-sigpipe-readers.sh
call: is_ignored "$f" && continue
why:  The only thing keeping the shell walk out of gitignored files. Removing it makes the gate red locally on files CI can never see, and green in CI, with nothing pointing at the difference.

path: scripts/check-shellcheck.sh
call: is_ignored "$f" && continue
why:  Same guard, same walk, same failure mode — a local-only RED naming a real violation in a file CI's clean clone does not have.

path: scripts/check-stale-references.sh
call: drop_ignored_lines
why:  The only filter between this gate's tree-wide grep and gitignored scratch output. Removing it makes downloaded fixtures and local reports look like tracked debt.

path: .github/workflows/governance.yml
call: - name: Scan every tracked file for invisible characters
why:  The only CI run of the invisible-character gate. check-gate-selftest-parity now fails when this step goes but the job keeps its --self-test step, yet delete the whole job and every parity gate stays green (neither can see a job that is gone) while a bidirectional control or zero-width character can land in source and nobody sees it.

path: .gitattributes
call: packages/furs/schemas/*.xsd -text
why:  The only thing stopping `* text=auto` from normalizing the vendored FURS schemas' CRLF line endings on commit. Delete it and the furs SHA-256 test stays green (the working tree keeps its bytes); the next re-vendored schema is then committed normalized, CI's checkout no longer matches its pin, and the easy "fix" pins bytes FURS never published.

path: website/astro.config.mjs
call: markdownLinks(),
why:  The only wiring of the Markdown link rewrite. Delete it and the build, page-size check and link check stay green while every in-content link on the deployed Pages site 404s and repository links ignore GITHUB_REPOSITORY.

path: apps/web/vite.config.ts
call: plugins: [react(), contentSecurityPolicy()],
why:  The only wiring of the web app's Content Security Policy. Drop contentSecurityPolicy() and every test, lint and build stays green while the shipped page loses its script-src 'self' and connect-src 'self' protection.

path: apps/web/src/App.tsx
call: target?.focus({ preventScroll: true });
why:  The only focus move after a screen change. Without it keyboard and screen-reader users stay on a button that no longer exists; the tests render without a DOM, so nothing notices.

path: apps/web/src/screens/FilesStep.tsx
call: listHeading.current?.focus();
why:  Keeps focus in the file list after a removal deletes the focused button. Delete it and focus falls to the page body with every test still green.

path: apps/web/src/App.tsx
call: if (!listed.has(id)) bytes.current.delete(id);
why:  The only place a file's bytes leave memory once the user removes it, swaps it for the demo or starts over (ADR 0013 §6). Delete it and they stay in the tab until it closes; the screen tests render without effects and cannot see it.

path: apps/web/src/App.tsx
call: window.addEventListener("beforeunload", ask);
why:  The only warning before a reload or a closed tab loses the user's files (ADR 0013 §6). Delete it and they vanish without a word; the screen tests render without effects and cannot see it.

path: apps/web/src/App.tsx
call: resetKey={shownScreen}
why:  The only boundary around the wizard's screens (its reset key; the dashboard's shell and pages, and the tour, have their own): without it a render error blanks the whole app, and the user's files and answers go with it. The screen tests render on the server, where a boundary never catches, so deleting it leaves every test green.

path: apps/web/src/App.tsx
call: resetKey={`shell:${shownScreen}`}
why:  The boundary around the dashboard's shell (#47): its navigation, the returns writer and the counts render outside the pages' own boundaries. Without it a failure there blanks the app, files and answers with it; the screen tests render on the server, where a boundary never catches, so deleting it leaves every test green.

path: apps/web/src/screens/dashboard/DashboardShell.tsx
call: resetKey={`dashboard:${page}`}
why:  The boundary around the dashboard's page header, attention note and state notes (#47), keeping the phone's Back and Start over in its fallback. Delete it and a failure there falls to the shell's boundary, which drops the navigation; nothing in the tests catches, since they render on the server.

path: apps/web/src/screens/dashboard/DashboardShell.tsx
call: resetKey={`dashboard-page:${shown}`}
why:  The boundary around each of the dashboard's pages (#47): a page that fails to render, shown or hidden, gives way to a message while the other pages, the downloads on the overview and the user's files stay. The screens' own boundary in App.tsx wraps the wizard steps only. The tests render on the server, where a boundary never catches, so deleting it leaves every test green.

path: apps/web/src/App.tsx
call: onError={() => {
why:  The only way a failure inside the guided tour ends it. The tour's boundary renders nothing in its place, so without this handler the tour stays open with no dialog: its stop keeps overriding the screen the user navigates to, the screen-change focus move stays off, and the user's view is never given back (ADR 0016). No test makes the tour throw, so deleting it leaves every test green.

path: apps/web/content-security-policy.mjs
call: "worker-src 'self' blob:",
why:  The only grant for the engine's blob: bootstrap, which makes the worker inherit the page's policy (ADR 0013 §3). Drop blob: and every test and build stays green while the deployed app can no longer start its engine, so no file of the user's is read.

path: apps/web/content-security-policy.mjs
call: "frame-src 'none'",
why:  The only thing that stops the app from framing a page: without it default-src 'self' lets the app frame any page on its origin, which it shares with these docs and every Pages site of the account (ADR 0009). The Pages build check compares the built tag with this module, so it follows the deletion, and no test or e2e spec names the directive: delete it and everything stays green.

path: .github/workflows/ci.yml
call: pnpm --dir apps/web exec playwright test
why:  The only place CI runs the browser tests: the guided tour's keyboard path, focus and scroll restore, no sideways scroll at 320px, and the zero-violation check of the Content Security Policy on the built app. Delete it and the e2e job passes having run nothing.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: node apps/web/scripts/pages-build/check-pages-build.mjs apps/web/dist "$WEB_BASE"
why:  The only run of the Pages build check on every pull request and push (job web-app); website-build runs it again only when the app is published. check-gate-selftest-parity now fails when this line goes but the step keeps its --self-test line, yet delete the whole step and every parity gate stays green (neither can see a step that is gone) while a wrong base, a lost Content Security Policy tag, an inline script or a stray page goes unreported until the day PUBLISH_WEB_APP is set.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: node apps/web/scripts/pages-build/check-pages-build.mjs "$WEB_APP_DIR" "$WEB_BASE"
why:  The only check of the copy that actually joins the Pages artifact (job website-build, when the app is published, run just before the copy), and with it the refusal of any file but index.html and the scripts, stylesheets and fonts under assets/. Delete it and the step runs only the self-test, which keeps check-gate-selftest-parity green, while whatever web-app's build handed over (a page with no policy, a weakened policy) is published unchecked; the shell refusal beside it catches links and other non-files only.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: odd="$(find "$WEB_APP_DIR" ! -type f ! -type d)"
why:  website-build's walk of the whole downloaded app for links and other non-files, before the checker reads it and the copy publishes it (upload-pages-artifact archives with tar --dereference, so a link would publish what it points at). Today the checker refuses the same entries too, at the root and in assets/, and refuses every other directory, so deleting this line leaves every run green and the artifact still refused. It is the only refusal that does not depend on the checker's list of allowed places: widen that list (a subdirectory of assets/, say) after deleting it, and a link there is published with every gate green.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: if: steps.publish.outputs.app == 'true' && steps.recheck.outcome == 'success'
why:  The only guard on the step that copies the web app into the Pages artifact: only when it is published, and only after its re-check itself succeeded (continue-on-error on the re-check would not let it through). Delete it while PUBLISH_WEB_APP is true, as it is once the app is live, and every run stays green, so the merge goes through; the day the variable is unset to take the app down, the copy fails on the download that no longer happens, website-build goes red, and the takedown is never deployed, so the app stays published. With the variable unset, deleting it is red at once.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: id: recheck
why:  The id the copy step's guard reads (steps.recheck.outcome). Delete or rename it and that outcome reads empty, so the copy never runs: with PUBLISH_WEB_APP true, website-build stays green and uploads docs whose sidebar links an app/ that is not in the artifact (the sidebar check compares the link with the variable, not with what was copied), and the next deploy takes the app down. actionlint reports the undefined step, but only where someone runs it before a push; no CI job or pre-push gate does.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: if: ${{ !cancelled() && needs.detect.outputs.site == 'true' }}
why:  Keeps website-build running when web-app or base-path fails (but not in a cancelled run), so its first step can fail it. Delete !cancelled() and a failed web-app skips website-build, a skipped required check counts as passed, and a lost Content Security Policy or a wrong base path no longer blocks the merge.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: if [ "$BASE_PATH_RESULT" != "success" ] || [ "$WEB_APP_RESULT" != "success" ]; then
why:  The only thing that turns a failed web-app or base-path into a red website-build, the job branch protection requires. Delete this if block (or the whole step) and website-build builds the docs and passes while web-app is red, so the merge goes through. Deleting only this line is a shell syntax error, red at once; the stanza names the if line, as the guards above do, so either deletion fails this gate.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: if [ "$linked" != "$PUBLISHED" ]; then
why:  The only check that the docs sidebar links the web app exactly when the app is in the Pages artifact. Delete the comparison (this if block, or the whole step) and every run stays green while the sidebar can link an app that is not published, a 404, or the app can be published with no link to it. Deleting only this line is a shell syntax error, red at once; the stanza names the if line, as the guards above do, so either deletion fails this gate.

path: .github/workflows/docs.yml
call: if [ "${PUBLISH_WEB_APP:-}" != "true" ]; then
why:  The only thing keeping the web app out of the Pages artifact until PUBLISH_WEB_APP is true. Delete the guard and the next push to main publishes the app with every gate green.
